# G100 — Versioning Manager

*v2. See `versioning-manager.md` for prose: content-addressed storage
(blobs/trees/commits keyed by hash), commit chain as linked list of
trees, canonical encoding as the cross-language byte-identity contract,
tree diff as hash equality, automatic dedup by construction.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault History

[vault-history
    IN ORDER TO
        record every vault save as a commit in a content-addressed repo
        answer per-file history queries by walking the commit chain
        and restore historical state by checking out any commit

    @repo <- [vm[new-repo]]

    sync @vault-events {
        save @path @content @message ->
            @files <- [vault[list-tracked]]
                       mapped to (@f, [vault[read-bytes {path: @f:path}]])
            @files <- @files overriding where @f:path is @path
                             with @content
            [vm[commit {repo: @repo,
                         files: @files,
                         message: @message,
                         timestamp: [clock[now]]}]]

        file-history @file ->
            @commits <- [vm[log {repo: @repo}]]
                           where [vm[checkout
                                      {repo: @repo,
                                       commit-hash: @c:hash}]]
                                 has key @file
            [ui[render-history {commits: @commits}]]

        restore @commit-hash ->
            @snapshot <- [vm[checkout
                              {repo: @repo,
                               commit-hash: @commit-hash}]]
                || -> #unknown-commit @commit-hash
            for (@path, @bytes) in @snapshot
                [vault[write-bytes {path: @path, content: @bytes}]]
    }
]

---

## Verb Surface

[vm
    fnv1a-hash          {bytes}
    new-repo
    commit              {repo, files, message, timestamp}    ;; -> Hash
    checkout            {repo, commit-hash}                   ;; || unknown-commit
    diff                {repo, from, to}                       ;; -> [DiffOp]
    log                 {repo}                                 ;; -> [(Hash, Commit)]
    blob-count          {repo}
    commit-count        {repo}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: identity as `where hash(data) = key`

[vm[commit {repo: @r, files: @fs, ...}]
    IN ORDER TO
        store each blob/tree/commit under its own content hash
        so identical content collapses to one object automatically

    for each (name, bytes) in @fs
        @hash <- [vm[fnv1a-hash {bytes: @bytes}]]
        @r:blobs at @hash
            where already present -> reuse (no new storage)
            otherwise             -> insert bytes under @hash
]

The identifier IS the content. Same bytes → same hash → same blob.
"Check if this already exists" vanishes because the hash already
answered that question. Storage dedup is emergent, not coded.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: canonical encoding as cross-language contract

[vm[tree-to-bytes {tree: @t}]
    IN ORDER TO
        produce the exact byte sequence every Rosetta language agrees on
        so the same files produce the same hash across Rust/Python/CL/Go/
        Lean/InnateScript

    bytes are "tree\n"
    followed by, for each entry ordered by name ascending,
        name + "\t" + lowercase-hex(hash, 16 digits) + "\n"
    where hex is lowercase
    and newlines are "\n"
    and fields are separated by "\t"
]

This is the milestone's strongest cross-language contract: not just
same logic, not just same output, but **identical bytes** produced by
six independent implementations from the same input.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: diff as `where tree hashes differ`

[vm[diff {repo: @r, from: @a, to: @b}]
    @a:tree and @b:tree
        where both hashes are equal
            -> [] (identical; no file reads needed)
        otherwise, for each name in either tree
            where only in @b    -> #added
            where only in @a    -> #removed
            where hash differs  -> #changed
]

Two commits are identical iff their trees hash identically — a single
64-bit comparison replaces a full file-by-file walk. This is the
algorithmic win that makes content-addressed storage worth its weight.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @r  <- [vm[new-repo]]
    @c1 <- [vm[commit
                 {repo: @r,
                  files: [["a.txt", "hello"], ["b.txt", "world"]],
                  message: "initial", timestamp: 1000}]]
    @c2 <- [vm[commit
                 {repo: @r,
                  files: [["a.txt", "hello!"],
                           ["b.txt", "world"],
                           ["c.txt", "new"]],
                  message: "update a, add c", timestamp: 2000}]]

    -> [vm[diff {repo: @r, from: @c1, to: @c2}]]
    ;; -> [#changed a.txt, #added c.txt]

    -> [vm[checkout {repo: @r, commit-hash: @c1}]]
    ;; -> {a.txt: "hello", b.txt: "world"}

    -> [vm[blob-count {repo: @r}]]
    ;; -> 4   (hello, world, hello!, new — automatic dedup)
]
